#import "template.typ": *

#let metadata = (
  title: "Diversions & Lost Procedure",
  unit: "Cross Country Endorsement | Lesson 5",
  author: "C.Moroney",
  version: "v1.0.0",
)

#let lesson = (
title: [#metadata.title]

,aim: [
To divert the aircraft from the planned track — both temporarily (around weather or terrain) and to a new destination — and to carry out the lost procedure to re-establish position. To introduce low-level navigation by demonstration as context for the next lesson.
]


,overview: [
This lesson addresses elements *1.5 Perform lost procedure* (fix position, calculate new track, determine a diversion point within fuel and daylight limitations, calculate revised ETA, use simulated radio for assistance, employ nav aids/landmarks), *1.6 Plan and perform diversion*, *1.2 Notification and SARWATCH*, and introduces *1.4 Low level navigation* by demonstration. There is a lot of content here, so it may be split across two flights. The student should divert around simulated weather/terrain using the 60-degree (equal-heading) or 1-in-60 method, divert to an unplanned new destination, and practise the lost procedure. The instructor demonstrates low-level navigation so the student has context to practise next lesson. The route may be complex; much of it will not be flown because the focus is diversions.
]

,learning_outcomes: [
#text(size:12pt)[*Knowledge*]
- Recall the method for a temporary diversion left/right of track for weather or terrain.
- Recall the procedure for diverting to a new aerodrome: hold, draw, *check airspace/terrain*, estimate heading, turn onto heading and take time, measure distance, calculate ETI/ETA, CLEAROFF.
- Recall the lost procedure: maintain heading, "reverse" DR position, area of probability, read ground-to-chart, use ATC/nav aids, log time and fuel; precautionary search and landing if required.
- Explain flight notification and SARWATCH (submission and cancellation).
- Explain the concept of determining a diversion point within fuel and daylight limitations.
#text(size:12pt)[*Skills*]
- Divert the aircraft temporarily off track and regain the original track.
- Plan and fly a diversion to a new, unplanned destination/town, including revised ETA.
- Carry out the lost procedure to re-establish position.
- Submit a flight notification to NAIPS and brief SAR information.
#text(size:12pt)[*Non-Technical Skills*]
- [TEM] Apply ADM: decide early, don't press on into deteriorating weather.
- [HF] Manage the stress of being "lost"; work the procedure methodically.
- [SA] Maintain SA and a running position estimate at all times.
- [CRM] Use simulated radio assistance and available nav aids appropriately.
]

,theory_knowledge: [
- Lost procedure *(Pg 259)*
- Restricted and Danger areas *(Pg 258–259)*
- Decision-making — false hypothesis, mind-set *(Pg 34–35)*
- v8 Theory Unit 2.03 §1.1.7 (in-flight diversion principles, decision-making for diversions around adverse weather, diversion to an alternate aerodrome; establishing a DR position; map reading for the lost procedure).
]

,ground_brief_topics: [
Total time: 60 mins (may split over two flights) \
Equipment required: VNC, ERSA, plotter, E6B, EFB, nav log, NAIPS access, current GAF/GPWT and NOTAM \

*Non-Technical Skills* (5 minutes)
- [TEM] ADM and weather avoidance — decide early.
- [HF] Managing the stress of being unsure of position.
- [CRM] Using radio assistance and nav aids.

*Temporary diversion* (5 minutes)
- Diverting left/right of track for weather/terrain; regaining track (1-in-60 / equal-heading).

*Diversion to a new destination* (15 minutes)
- Procedure: 1. HOLD in a safe area. 2. DRAW present position to diversion point. 3. *CHECK ROUTE* CTA/SUA/Terrain; 4. Estimate Heading; 5. Exit hold, note time; 6. Measure distance and calculate ETI, note ETA; 7. CLEAROFF and brief the arrival.

*Lost procedure* (15 minutes)
- Maintain heading; establish DR position and an area of probability; read ground-to-chart; use ATC/nav aids; log time and fuel; precautionary search and landing if required.

*Flight notification and SARWATCH* (10 minutes)
- Submitting to NAIPS; SAR briefing; cancellation responsibilities.

*Low-level navigation (intro)* (10 minutes)
- Concept and risks; LSALT; slowing the aircraft; following a feature; precautionary search and landing — to be demonstrated in flight.
]

,ground_tasks:[
#text(size:12pt)[*Instructor*]
- Review weather, NOTAM and fuel; select a route that supports diversion practice.
- Prepare scenarios for temporary diversion, new-destination diversion and lost procedure.
- Be prepared to take control for the first few diversions and to demonstrate low-level navigation.
#text(size:12pt)[*Student*]
- Complete IMSAFE; brief the go/no-go decision.
- Prepare a complete nav log (TR, HDG, ETI, FUEL, frequencies).
- Submit a flight plan to NAIPS and brief SAR information.
]

,in_flight_tasks:[
#text(size:12pt)[*Demonstration*]
- A diversion to a new destination using the hold/draw/estimate/measure/calculate method.
- The lost procedure (if the student becomes genuinely unsure, or set up artificially).
- Low-level navigation to a destination, emphasising always knowing position on the chart.
#text(size:12pt)[*Student Practice*]
- Leg 1: temporarily divert around simulated weather and regain track.
- Leg 2: divert to a new destination/town with assistance.
- Practise the lost procedure if the student overshoots or becomes unsure (set up artificially if needed).
- Determine a diversion point within fuel and daylight limitations and calculate a revised ETA.
- Use simulated radio assistance where appropriate.
]

,common_errors:[
- *Diverting before holding*: Establish a stable hold first, then plan — don't navigate and turn simultaneously.
- *Skipping the airspace/terrain check*: Always check CTA/PRD and terrain on the new track before turning.
- *Pressing on into weather*: Decide early; a precautionary search and landing is always an option.
- *Reading chart-to-ground when lost*: When lost, read ground-to-chart within an area of probability.
- *Forgetting fuel/daylight limits*: The diversion point must sit inside fuel and daylight margins.
- *Forgetting SARWATCH*: Brief and manage SAR; remember cancellation on arrival.
]

,post_flight:[
- *Student Self-Assessment*: What went well; how the lost/diversion procedures felt under workload.
- *Instructor Feedback* (10 min):
  - Evaluate against the standards below (S / C / NC).
  - Highlight strengths; nominate focus areas.
  - Discuss ADM and TEM (eg. timing of the weather-avoidance decision).
- *Scenario Reflection*: "You're unsure of position with 45 minutes of fuel and 60 minutes to last light — what's your decision sequence?"
- *Next Steps*: Assign homework. See below.
]

,standards_for_progression:[
- Uses 1-in-60 effectively; revises ETA (when required) to within ±3 minutes.
- Position-fixes quickly and accurately.
- Maintains straight-and-level ±100 ft and heading ±5°.
- Operates the nav log on standard flights and diversions; manages the fuel log and frequencies.
- Recalls and implements CLEAROFF; arrives at and conducts a touch-and-go at a foreign aerodrome without assistance.
- Plans and flies a diversion to a new destination and recalls the lost procedure.
- Submits a flight notification to NAIPS and manages SARWATCH.
- *Competency stage (v8)*: record S / C / NC. Diversion planning and lost procedure assessed toward C (competent, pre-flight-test).
]

,safety:[
- *Weather*: Visible horizon; ceiling/visibility comfortably above VFR minima. Real deteriorating weather is *avoided* — diversions around weather are simulated.
- *Altitude*: En route minimum 1,500 ft AGL. The low-level *demonstration* is conducted no lower than 500 ft AGL, over suitable terrain, by the instructor.
- *Emergency Procedures*: Pre-select forced-landing and precautionary-search options; brief precautionary search and landing.
- *TEM*:
  - Manage student overload during the high-task diversion/lost exercises — pause and prioritise aviate.
  - Maintain a buffer from CTA/PRD even while diverting.
  - Take control for early diversions.
  - No lower than 500 AGL - avoid overflying houses, do not overfly townships or airfields.
- *Suggested Routes*: YHEC-YMUR-YLIS (TGL)-Rathdowney - Low level - Tamborine-YHEC | YHEC-YWCK-YSRA - Low Level - Bromelton-YHEC
- *Aircraft Limits*: Operate within POH/AFM; configure appropriately for any slowed/low-level segment.
]

,homework:[
- Read Bob Tait's RAAus BAK:
  - Lost procedure (Pg 259); Restricted and Danger areas (Pg 258–259); decision-making (Pg 34–35).
- Prepare for next lesson (*Low Level / Consolidation*): review LSALT, low-level hazards and meteorological hazards (Pg 262–266).
- Practice: plan a diversion to an alternate from a mid-route point, including revised ETA and a fuel/daylight check.
]
)

#show: body => lesson_plan(metadata, lesson, body)
